Universal Basic Income (UBI) should be implemented as it offers a robust solution to modern economic challenges. By providing economic security, UBI ensures a financial safety net for all citizens, which directly reduces poverty, the very essence of economic inequality. This reduction of poverty is a direct result of the economic security UBI provides. Furthermore, with technological advancements causing inevitable job displacement, UBI must be adopted to support workers who face unemployment due to automation. This proactive measure will offer them the capacity to retrain and adapt to new opportunities, reinforcing economic security. Additionally, UBI simplifies the welfare system by eliminating the need for multiple overlapping programs, thus reducing bureaucratic inefficiencies. The resultant savings from streamlined welfare can then be reinvested to further enhance economic security for all. Therefore, UBI should be embraced not only as a means to provide economic security and reduce poverty but as a fundamental restructuring of welfare systems to foster a more equitable and resilient society. Implementing UBI ultimately supports its own necessity, as it addresses economic security and job displacement, which it aims to solve.

