Ranked-choice voting (RCV) enhances the democratic process by promoting majority support, reducing negative campaigning, and encouraging a more diverse range of candidates. By allowing voters to rank their preferences, RCV ensures that elected officials have majority backing, considering secondary choices and leading to more widely accepted outcomes. This system also fosters more civil discourse as candidates aim to appeal to a broader audience, reducing negative campaigning and focusing on constructive dialogue. Furthermore, RCV encourages a broader range of candidates, including those from minor parties, by allowing voters to express their preferences without fear of wasting their vote. Therefore, RCV inherently enhances democracy and leads to more representative electoral outcomes.

