[Essay]  
While the fundamental right to free expression is a cornerstone of democratic society, it is imperative to implement targeted regulations on hate speech to protect marginalized communities from violence and discrimination. The false assumption that regulating hate speech automatically leads to comprehensive censorship fails to acknowledge the nuanced approach necessary to foster an inclusive and equitable public discourse. Hate speech has a demonstrable correlation with increased discrimination and violence against vulnerable groups. Therefore, regulation should be in place to mitigate these harmful effects. A policy that enforces clear guidelines on what constitutes hate speech can protect marginalized communities while maintaining the essence of free expression. By comparing societies with hate speech regulations to those without, it becomes apparent that regulations do not erode democratic freedoms but support a more harmonious society. Thus, governments should enact sensible regulations, focusing on limiting speech promoting racism, sexism, and other forms of bigotry. These policies will help create safer environments for all citizens. It is our collective responsibility to ensure that the right to free expression does not come at the cost of another's safety and dignity.

