[Essay]
Transitioning from a First-Past-The-Post (FPTP) voting system to a Proportional Representation (PR) system is essential for creating a more equitable and truly representative democracy. PR ensures fairer representation of diverse political views by accurately reflecting the electorate’s preferences and allowing minority parties to have a voice proportional to their share of the vote. This shift encourages the emergence of new political parties, breaking the stronghold of a two-party system and fostering a more vibrant political landscape. Furthermore, PR leads to higher voter engagement by making every vote matter, as people feel their votes contribute directly to the legislature's makeup. Critics may argue that FPTP provides stability and strong local representation, yet they fail to acknowledge the inherent flaws in ignoring minority voices and discouraging political diversity. By focusing solely on stability, FPTP advocates overlook the fact that true democratic stability arises from fair representation and inclusion, not from excluding emerging parties. Thus, transitioning to a PR system is not just a reform but a necessary evolution towards a democracy where every voice and vote count.

