[Essay]  
The reliance on standardized testing as a primary method of evaluating student achievement narrows education and exacerbates inequities. While such tests purportedly offer an objective metric for comparison, they fall short in capturing the full spectrum of student abilities. This reliance on standardized assessments can lead to a restricted focus, as educators might "teach to the test" rather than fostering a broad educational experience. Consequently, this undermines critical thinking and creativity, reducing education to mere test preparation. Additionally, standardized tests disproportionately disadvantage students who face high-pressure testing challenges, including those with learning differences or linguistic barriers. In an equitable education system, students from under-resourced schools should have the same opportunities for success. Thus, a more balanced approach that incorporates alternative assessment methods, like portfolio assessments and project-based learning, is essential. These methods offer a holistic view of student abilities, fostering creativity and critical thinking. Education policies must prioritize diverse talents and real-world skills application, moving beyond narrow metrics of standardized testing to nurture each student's potential fully.

