[Essay]  
While national security is undeniably important, prioritizing extensive government surveillance over individual privacy constitutes a dangerous infringement on civil liberties. Analogous to a doctor prescribing medication, while medicine can cure, overprescription without oversight can lead to harmful side effects. In the same way, unchecked surveillance can lead to abuse of power, stifling dissent and infringing on democratic rights. Factual evidence shows documented misuse of surveillance powers by governments, targeting political opponents and minority groups. This highlights the need for a shift towards targeted surveillance strategies. Just as doctors use precise diagnostics for specific ailments, surveillance should be focused and justified, preventing overreach. Robust oversight mechanisms, akin to medical boards reviewing prescriptions, are essential to ensure accountability and protect democratic freedoms. Furthermore, technological solutions like encryption can balance security needs with privacy concerns. Empirical data suggest that mass surveillance has not conclusively proven effective in preventing major threats, reinforcing the need for more strategic approaches. Ultimately, protecting public safety should not come at the expense of the core democratic value of individual privacy.

