Multiple implausible scenarios arise when considering AI's role in law enforcement. Imagine AI not only predicting crimes before they occur but also arresting individuals based solely on potential future actions, leading to the elimination of due process. Equally implausible is the notion that AI could completely eradicate bias, given that it is designed by humans and trained on imperfect data. Furthermore, the idea that AI will operate flawlessly without oversight is far-fetched, neglecting the reality of technological malfunctions. Lastly, envision AI systems being so advanced that they could independently judge and sentence individuals without human intervention, which presents a dystopian view of justice devoid of human empathy. These layers of implausibility highlight the need for caution and restraint in adopting AI within the justice system.

